I started this assignment by using the integrated userinfo command on the
terminal to see my own data. then I began working on the code.

For Homework 1 and 2, I used Agile TDD, where I would write the test cases 
first, then implement code and test it, upload to github, then continue. 
For this assignment, I used waterfall. I wrote all of the code, then tested.

This method of development had its pros and cons. I am much more familiar
with Waterfall as that was my main method of coding for my entire academic
career. however, for long code that is several hundred lines, TDD is better.

This program came out to around 160 lines.

I started by implimenting the switch cases for the -e and -n flag, then I 
added all of the easy outputs that did not require extra code like 
class, uid, username, etc.

I then wrote the logic for everything else, checking how many groups a given
user was a part of, checking when a user would expire, etc.

One specific issue I encountered was that I was unable to compile on my local
system, so I had to do all of my initial testing on the ssh server. 

getgrouplist() expects an int array, while NetBSD expects a gid_t array. 
Since NetBSD is the target system for thisassignment, I compiled and tested 
my program on the SSH server instead.

Overall, this assignment not too challenging and was a good practice to develop
actual usable software.